#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054987 :question/math-academy-id "q-1210" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the sets $A={x\\in Z:-2\\le x<6}$ and $B={x\\in N:x+1<6}?$\n\n- $∅⊆B$\n- $B⊆A$\n- $A⊈B$" :question/worked-solution "First, we must find the elements of the sets $A$ and $B.$\n\n- The elements of the set $A$ are all integer numbers greater than or equal to $-2$ but less than $6.$ So,\n\n$$\nA={-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4,5}.\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the elements of the set $B$ are natural numbers that satisfy the inequality $x+1<6.$ So,\n\n$$\nB={1,2,3,4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's inspect the given statements.\n\n- $∅⊆B$ is true. The null set $∅={}$ is a subset of *every* set.\n\n- $B⊆A$ is true. Every element in the set $B$ is an element of the set $A.$\n\n- $A⊈B$ is true. For example, $-1\\in A$ but $-1\\notin B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064586 :question/math-academy-id "q-21528" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{5}+5{x}^{3}-90x+108.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(-\\sqrt{3})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{″}(-\\sqrt{3})<0$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative minimum at $x=-\\sqrt{3}$\n- $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-\\sqrt{3}$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=-\\sqrt{3}$ is a stationary point of $f(x).$ We will use the second derivative test to classify this stationary point.\n\nCalculating the first derivative for the function $f(x)={x}^{5}+5{x}^{3}-90x+108,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=5{x}^{4}+15{x}^{2}-90.\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we get \n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=20{x}^{3}+30x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\sqrt{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-\\sqrt{3}) & =20{(-\\sqrt{3})}^{3}+30(-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =20\\cdot 3(-\\sqrt{3})+30(-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =-90\\sqrt{3}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, we conclude that $f(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=-\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087989 :question/math-academy-id "q-11718" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- For the matrix $A$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(A)=0\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 1=-1.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(A)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The matrix $B$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n\n- For the matrix $C$, we have\n\n$$\ndet(C)=1\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 1=0.\n$$\n\nBecause $det(C)=0,$ we conclude that $C$ is not invertible. Since $C$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $C$ is singular.\n\nTherefore, only $C$ is singular."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106297 :question/math-academy-id "q-36792" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =6.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (but not $2$).\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=0v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(3v)=3Av=3(2v)=2(3v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(3v)=2(3v),$ and $3v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2$ (but not $6$).\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115837 :question/math-academy-id "q-42265" :question/problem "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $4\\times 4$ matrices, which of the following statements are always true?\n\n- $AB=BA$\n- $(AB)C=A(BC)$\n- $AC+BC=(A+B)C$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $AB\\ne BA$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is associative, so $(AB)C=A(BC)$ in general.\n\n- Statement III is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+B)C=AC+BC,$ and $A(B+C)=AB+AC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statements II and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152751 :question/math-academy-id "q-76830" :question/problem "Given that $23≡11(modn)$, which of the following could be the value of $n$?\n\n1. $n = 3$\n2. $n = 2$\n3. $n = 5$"}]}
